      "head_commit": {
        "id": "c88c500de97d302a53980a34ccb9ec4948e3a717",
        "tree_id": "b9d61a72433a74a7968cb887ab0267c8fb2b3ff3",
        "message": "show Chinese instead of boxes, and translate the interface\n\nAndroid's font backend hands Slint no CJK fallback: fontique builds its\nscript -> family map from /system/etc/fonts.xml, but Android declares its\nChinese, Japanese and Korean families by file name\n(`<family lang=\"zh-Hans\"><font>NotoSansCJK-Regular.ttc</font></family>`), and\nthose entries are skipped. Nothing maps the Han script to a font that covers\nit, so with no fallback left to try every Chinese character came out as an\nempty box. The strings themselves were always correct UTF-8, which is why the\nsame text showed fine on the desktop peer it was sent to. src/fonts.rs points\nHani (which fontique appends to every query) at the CJK families the system\nalready reports.\n\nThe interface is translatable now, Simplified Chinese included. Slint bundles\ntranslations/<lang>/LC_MESSAGES/*.po into the binary, and src/i18n.rs gives the\nstrings Rust formats itself a lookup table generated from the same catalogs,\nsince Slint's own translations only cover @tr(...) in .slint files. Settings\ngained a Language choice (System / English / 简体中文) defaulting to the system\nlocale: file transfer statuses, toasts, history rows and dialogs all follow it.\n\nAlso adds a workflow that builds a signed APK on every push to master and\npublishes it to an `android-latest` pre-release, because the upstream release\npipeline only runs on version tags.",
        "timestamp": "2026-10-16T07:23:59Z",
